      "id": "1.1",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "html": "<p>Survival analysis describes the time until a specified event, which need not be death or an adverse outcome. Recovery, remission, conception, and cessation of smoking can also be events. The clock and endpoint must be defined together: time since treatment is a different outcome from age at death, even when both records end at the same death.</p><h3>The minimum data description</h3><ul><li><strong>Population and sampling:</strong> who can enter the study, how they are selected, and whether observations are independent, matched, or clustered.</li><li><strong>Time origin:</strong> birth, diagnosis, remission, transplantation, or another defined starting point. Distinguish calendar date from elapsed time.</li><li><strong>Event:</strong> a precisely specified outcome; a composite endpoint may combine several outcomes.</li><li><strong>Observation:</strong> an exact event time, last known event-free time, or an interval containing the event. Record the event indicator separately from the time.</li><li><strong>Covariates:</strong> baseline attributes and measurements or intermediate events that change during follow-up.</li><li><strong>Estimand:</strong> overall survival, event-free survival, a cause-specific probability, a group contrast, or prognosis conditional on covariates.</li></ul><div class=\"formula\">Y = min(T, C), &nbsp; δ = I(T ≤ C)<br>T: latent event time; C: right-censoring time; Y: observed follow-up time.</div><p>For a person censored at c, we know T &gt; c. Their event time is not zero, not c, and not missing in the same sense as a completely unobserved record. Deleting them discards known follow-up; treating c as a failure introduces events that were never observed.</p><h3>The observation mechanisms introduced by the examples</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Information available</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Right censoring</td><td>Included subject; event occurs after a boundary.</td></tr><tr><td>Left censoring</td><td>Included subject; event occurred before a boundary.</td></tr><tr><td>Interval censoring</td><td>Included subject; event lies between two observations.</td></tr><tr><td>Left truncation</td><td>Subjects who fail before entry are absent from the sample.</td></tr><tr><td>Right truncation</td><td>Subjects whose events occur after the sampling cutoff are absent.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"note\">Reading focus: Sections 1.2–1.19 introduce study designs and recurring datasets. They are not a collection of independent statistical estimators. The notes below identify the endpoint, observation scheme, covariates, and methodological lesson for every example.</p>",
