      "title": "Remission Duration from a Clinical Trial for Acute Leukemia",
      "html": "<h3>Study question and clock</h3><p>Remission begins; follow to leukemia relapse, measured in months.</p><h3>Design and observation</h3><p>42 children were matched within hospitals by remission status and randomized within pairs to 6-MP or placebo.</p><p><strong>Observed information.</strong> Exact relapse times and right-censored remission durations; the textbook uses + to mark censored records.</p><p><strong>Recorded predictors.</strong> Treatment, remission status, and the matching structure.</p><h3>What to learn from this example</h3><p>Learn why a longer observed time with + is only a lower bound on remission duration. Preserve pairs when comparing treatment effects.</p><p>The example returns in product-limit and Nelson–Aalen estimation, mean survival and standard errors, Bayesian estimation, stratified log-rank testing, and stratified proportional hazards modeling.</p>",
