      "title": "Bone Marrow Transplantation for Leukemia",
      "html": "<h3>Study question and clock</h3><p>Transplantation starts the clock. Treatment failure combines relapse and death while still in remission.</p><h3>Design and observation</h3><p>A multicenter cohort of 137 leukemia transplant recipients illustrates a complex recovery process.</p><p><strong>Observed information.</strong> Right-censored follow-up; distinct failure causes; intermediate events may occur before failure.</p><p><strong>Recorded predictors.</strong> Baseline disease/risk group, recipient and donor characteristics, CMV status, waiting time, FAB group, hospital and prophylaxis; acute/chronic GVHD and platelet or granulocyte recovery can update history.</p><h3>What to learn from this example</h3><p>Relapse and death in remission compete for the first failure endpoint. Platelet recovery and GVHD can occur in different orders and alter subsequent prognosis; their future occurrence cannot be treated as known at transplantation.</p><p>This dataset connects survival/CIF estimation, risk-group comparisons, regression, time-dependent covariates, multistate models, and diagnostics. Keep the composite failure indicator separate from the event-type code.</p>",
