      "html": "<h3>Study question and clock</h3><p>Years from first treatment for laryngeal cancer to death.</p><h3>Design and observation</h3><p>90 men are classified into four ordered disease stages.</p><p><strong>Observed information.</strong> Exact death times or right censoring at the study end.</p><p><strong>Recorded predictors.</strong> Stage, age at diagnosis, and year of diagnosis.</p><h3>What to learn from this example</h3><p>An ordered stage trend is a different hypothesis from any difference among stages. Stage-by-age interaction asks whether the age association differs by stage.</p><p>Later uses include trend tests; global and local Cox tests; contrasts and confidence intervals; interaction; predicted survival; additive hazards; AFT model fitting and residual checks.</p>",
